          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does the thyroid do, and what goes wrong in hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "The thyroid gland's main job is to produce hormones, primarily thyroxine. This thyroxine is then changed in the body's tissues into a more active hormone called triiodothyronine. Together, these hormones control the metabolic rate of most tissues in your body (condition-hypothyroidism).\n\nIn hypothyroidism, the thyroid gland does not produce enough of these hormones (condition-hypothyroidism). Because these hormones regulate metabolism, insufficient levels lead to a slower metabolic rate.\n\nYour body has a feedback system involving the pituitary gland, which monitors thyroid hormone levels. If the thyroid is not making enough hormones, the pituitary gland releases more thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) to try and prompt the thyroid to produce more. This means that a failing thyroid often shows a rising TSH level even before the thyroid hormone levels themselves drop below the normal range (condition-hypothyroidism, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
